<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    RuitkMigrateSignalsAsmdef — the 0.21.0 signals-split codemod.

    0.21.0 moved Signal<T>, SignalFactory and SignalsRuntime out of Ruitk.Shared into
    their own assembly, Ruitk.Signals, so that a consumer can depend on signals WITHOUT
    pulling in the virtual DOM. Unity assembly references are not transitive for the
    compiler, so a user assembly definition that referenced Ruitk.Shared and named a
    signal type stops compiling until it also references Ruitk.Signals.

    This adds that reference to exactly the assembly definitions that need it: those
    that reference Ruitk.Shared (directly or via Ruitk.Runtime / Ruitk.Ugui /
    Ruitk.Editor) AND whose own sources name a signal type.

    It does NOT touch code that lives outside an assembly definition. Ruitk.Signals is
    autoReferenced, so the predefined assemblies (Assembly-CSharp and friends) already
    see it and never broke in the first place.

    Edits the references array TEXTUALLY rather than round-tripping the JSON: these
    files are hand-written and hand-read, and a reformat would bury the one-line change
    in a whole-file diff. Idempotent — a second run reports 0.

    Modeled on Tools/RuitkMigrateBrand. Not shipped to Unity (under a `~` tools folder).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AssemblyName>RuitkMigrateSignalsAsmdef</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Ruitk.SourceGenerator.Tools</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
